          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is it ever correct to submit a code root without its decimal characters?",
          "answer": "ICD-10-CM codes are typically three to seven characters long. The first three characters form the \"category\" or \"code root.\" A decimal point is used after the first three characters if there are more characters that follow.\n\nIt is sometimes correct to submit a code root without decimal characters. This happens when the three-character code is complete and provides enough information on its own, such as with code I10 for essential hypertension. However, most ICD-10-CM categories require additional characters to provide more specific details, like cause, anatomical site, or severity, and thus include a decimal point (Source: ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
